the countdown to Thanksgiving is on and  you know that means hours worth of  cooking but if you aren't careful  experts say all that work in the kitchen  could aggravate carpal tunnel paint  Victoria Saha spoke with local doctors  on tips to protect your hands  when you think of Thanksgiving you think  of the giant turkey and the delicious  sides that follow but before you get to  dig in there's lots of work that needs  to be done in the kitchen and for the  cook that means your hands and arms are  busy by the end of the night you could  be feeling numbness tingling and wrist  pain Dr Ryan grabo says at this time of  year is the perfect recipe for a carpal  tunnel pain we do have patients who come  in right after holidays or in prep for  holidays you're saying yeah I've been  making bread or cookies because I sent  them all over the country with my family  and now I'm having numbers continuing  but he says while you're in the middle  of chopping garnishing and mixing find  some time to do a couple of hand  stretches stretching wrist back for  about a minute bringing it down for  about a minute with your arm fully  extended this not only helps your carpal  tunnel but people have heard of tennis  elbow and golfers elbow Dr grumbo  recommends if you're someone that's  susceptible to wrist pain you could wear  a splint or brace while you're cooking  Victoria Saha eight news now the the  doctor says when it comes to carving  your turkey you'll want to put less  pressure on your hands by making sure  you have a sharp knife that will do the  majority of the work for you
 

